    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d340">

                <pb facs="0472" n="402" xml:id="pg402"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Dayton.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 229.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1862-10-03"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >October</hi> 3, 1862.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of September 13 (No. 195) has
                    been received. Your proceedings in calling Mr. Thouvenel’s attention to the many
                    rumors circulated by the press concerning Mr. Slidell’s advances towards the
                    French government seem to have been very discreet, and they are approved.
                    Without making it the occasion of a formal communication to Mr. Thouvenel, it is
                    proper that you should express in a casual manner to him the very favorable
                    impression which his frank and candid explanations have made upon the mind of
                    the President.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">William L. Dayton</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
